WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.380
Narrator: In 2017, researchers tested

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:05.340
20 best-selling US sunscreens.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.490
The good news is that 19
of them met FDA standards.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:10.690
The bad news?

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:13.555
Nine of them didn't
meet European standards.

00:00:13.555 --> 00:00:16.850
Turns out, different
countries have different rules

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.200
for what makes a safe sunscreen

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.200
and US sunscreens may not
be protecting Americans

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.373
as well as it could.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.160
When we lay in the sun,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.990
our skin absorbs two types of UV light.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.727
UVA and UVB rays.

00:00:34.727 --> 00:00:38.730
UVB light is higher energy
and can cause sunburns

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.780
while UVA penetrates deeper under the skin

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.340
and can damage skin cells
along the bottom layer

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.740
of your epidermis.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.264
Desai: We know that UVB rays

00:00:47.264 --> 00:00:49.256
are the rays that cause sunburns.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.030
But UVA rays are the rays
that can actually cause

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.380
skin cancer so you actually
wanna cover the spectrum

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:55.252
on both of those.

00:00:55.252 --> 00:00:57.610
I think a lot of people
get into a misconception

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.260
that I didn't get sunburned
so I'm not at prone

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.180
to getting skin cancer

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.260
which really isn't true.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.310
Narrator: The biggest
concern with US sunscreen

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:05.840
is how much protection you're getting

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.983
from cancer-causing UVA rays.

00:01:07.983 --> 00:01:11.440
For decades, FDA regulations
required that sunscreens

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.823
protect against UVB, but
not necessarily against UVA.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.680
Meanwhile, rates for melanoma,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.160
a dangerous form of skin
cancer, kept climbing in the US.

00:01:22.827 --> 00:01:26.310
Then, in 2012, the FDA
updated its regulations

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.660
on labeling and testing so
that manufacturers must now

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.720
let customers know if its
sunscreen protects against both

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.270
UVB and UVA.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.400
That's what the broad spectrum label

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.610
on your sunscreen means, for example.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:41.410
And while this is a good first step,

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.909
there's still no regulation
on how much protection

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:46.970
you're getting from UVA.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.630
So, there's no way to tell.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:49.640
Desai: Here in the United States,

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.300
I think we need to be
cognizant of the fact

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.510
that when a sunscreen
says it's broad spectrum,

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.750
UVA- and UVB-protecting, that does mean

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.180
you're going to get
protection against those rays.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.350
However, what it does not mean

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.830
is that it's going to
block out all of the rays.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.570
Narrator: And that's where
US sunscreens fall short.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.450
Desai: And I will say that
I do think we are behind

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:12.150
other countries globally,

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.830
particularly some of our
European counterparts,

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:17.460
in getting new sunscreen
ingredients approved.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.207
Overall, there has not been much change

00:02:20.207 --> 00:02:23.590
in US sunscreen composition
and what our sunscreens

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.690
are made up of in the past several years.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.890
Narrator: The FDA has
approved 16 active ingredients

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:31.606
that protect against UV radiation.

00:02:31.606 --> 00:02:33.113
But only some protect against

00:02:33.113 --> 00:02:36.946
both UVB and UVA rays.

00:02:36.946 --> 00:02:39.400
For comparison, Europe requires that all

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.436
of its more than 20 active
ingredients protect against both.

00:02:43.436 --> 00:02:45.830
Desai: Right now, the American
Academy of Dermatology

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:49.030
and other organizations are
really advocating with the FDA

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.106
that they need to really
speed up the approval process

00:02:52.106 --> 00:02:55.208
for new sunscreen ingredients.

00:02:55.208 --> 00:02:57.470
Because it's with these
ingredients that we can probably

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.650
get even better coverage and
better protection and maybe

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.120
even get something that's easier to apply,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.780
that's easier on the skin,

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.870
that doesn't have any harmful
side effects for patients.

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:09.258
Narrator: You can purchase sunscreens

00:03:09.258 --> 00:03:10.936
from other countries online.

00:03:10.936 --> 00:03:13.480
But if you plan on sticking
with American sunscreens,

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.440
look for the broad spectrum label

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.150
and don't buy anything below SPF 30.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.754
Desai: The higher the SPF,
definitely the better.

00:03:20.754 --> 00:03:23.840
But we definitely don't want
anyone going below a 30.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.390
And think about if you're
someone who has a history

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:28.340
of a melanoma, if you use an
SPF 30, you're blocking out

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.878
let's say 98% of the harmful rays.

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:33.982
However, what about the remaining 2%?

00:03:33.982 --> 00:03:37.420
That 2% may be something
that could be potential

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.370
of putting you at a risk down the road.

